---
// SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-only
// /privacy/: what this website and its public playground do with data. Website copy;
// the hosted data contract of the product is linked, not restated. A legal route: public and
// fetchable, but kept out of search engines, snippets and the site search.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import SectionHead from '../components/SectionHead.astro';
import { LIVE_PLAYGROUND_WORKER, docPaths, paths } from '../data/site';
---
<Base title="Privacy and data" description="What the diffdevil website and public playground do with data: no accounts, no analytics, no stored analyses; public pull requests only." current="privacy" noindex>
  <section class="section section-first">
    <article class="wrap legal">
      <SectionHead id="privacy" as="h1" title="Privacy and data" />
      <p class="lede">This page describes what the diffdevil website and its public playground do with data. The hosted GitHub App has its own <a class="link-quiet" href={docPaths.privacyAndData}>privacy and data contract</a>.</p>
      <h2 class="h3">The website</h2>
      <ul class="small">
        <li>Static pages, no accounts, no sign-in, no analytics, no tracking pixels, no third-party scripts.</li>
        <li>No cookies. Your theme choice (light, automatic or dark) is kept in the local storage of your browser under <code class="code-inline">diffdevil.theme</code> and never sent anywhere.</li>
        <li>Fonts (IBM Plex Sans and Mono) are served from this site; no request goes to a font network.</li>
        <li>Search runs in your browser over an index built with the site. What you type is not sent anywhere.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2 class="h3">The playground</h2>
      <ul class="small">
        <li>Curated examples are analyzed in your browser from files shipped with the site. Nothing is sent to a server when you open one or edit its policy.</li>
        <li>When you analyze a public pull request, your browser asks the playground API (<code class="code-inline">{LIVE_PLAYGROUND_WORKER}</code> in production) to read that public pull request from GitHub and return the analysis. The API accepts public <code class="code-inline">github.com</code> pull-request URLs only, never asks for a token, performs no repository writes, and stores no analysis history. The unauthenticated GitHub read allowance is shared by everyone using the API; when it is exhausted the API says so instead of inventing a result.</li>
        <li>The edited policy travels in the page URL so a result can be shared and reloaded. It is not stored by the site.</li>
        <li>"Carry into the GitHub App" passes the policy in the URL of the App page; nothing from the playground session is kept.</li>
        <li>Short-lived rate-limit and cache controls on the API are abuse and compute protection, not an activity timeline. Raw client addresses and entered URLs are not retained in product analytics.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2 class="h3">Agent Skill and setup instructions</h2>
      <p class="small">The skill and setup files are plain Markdown served from this site. Fetching them tells us nothing beyond an ordinary page request; the site does not identify the agent or the user.</p>
      <h2 class="h3">Contact and legal</h2>
      <p class="small">Legal notice: <a class="link-quiet" href={paths.impressum}>Impressum</a>. Security reports: <a class="link-quiet" href={paths.security}>Security</a>.</p>
    </article>
  </section>
</Base>
<style>
  .section-first { border-top: 0; padding-block: 40px 64px; }
  .legal { gap: 12px !important; }
  .legal > :global(*) { max-width: 760px; }
  .legal h2 { margin-top: 18px; }
  .legal ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 20px; display: grid; gap: 8px; color: var(--fg-2); }
  .legal p.small { margin: 0; color: var(--fg-2); }
</style>
